/* ============ MODULE 4 ============ */

/* ---- m4-types ---- */
Q('m4-types','e','Which is an example of procedural learning?',
 ['Learning to ride a bike','Remembering your tenth birthday','Knowing that Paris is in France','Getting used to a ticking clock'],
 'Procedural learning builds skills like cooking, biking, and typing. A birthday is episodic; Paris is semantic; the clock is habituation.');
Q('m4-types','a','After months of practice, a student types without looking at the keyboard. This skill came from',
 ['procedural learning','non-associative learning','classical conditioning','semantic memory'],
 'Building a skill like keyboarding through experience is procedural learning.');
Q('m4-types','r','Procedural learning leads to',
 ['procedural knowledge and skills','memories of personal life events','general knowledge of facts','reflexive conditioned responses'],
 'It results in procedural knowledge and skills (cooking, biking, typing).');
Q('m4-types','r','Implicit learning is the acquisition of information',
 ['without intent, in a way we cannot easily express','on purpose, through deliberate and repeated self-testing','by watching a model perform a behavior and copying it','by pairing a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned one'],
 'Implicit learning happens without intent and is hard to put into words.');
Q('m4-types','a','A person can write forward with one hand and backward with the other but cannot explain how they learned it or how they do it. This is an example of',
 ['implicit learning','observational learning','metacognition','elaboration'],
 'Acquired without intent and hard to express: implicit learning.');
Q('m4-types','r','Implicit memories are',
 ['long-term memories that do not require conscious thought to encode','short-term memories held only while working with information','memories of salient events from your own personal life','sensory memories that fade within a few seconds'],
 'Implicit learning produces implicit memories: long-term, encoded without conscious thought.');
Q('m4-types','r','Non-associative learning occurs when',
 ['a single repeated stimulus leads to a change in behavior','two stimuli are paired until one comes to predict the other','a behavior is followed by a consequence that changes its rate','a learner watches a social model and copies the behavior'],
 'One stimulus, repeated, with no pairing: non-associative learning.');
Q('m4-types','r','When a response decreases with repeated exposure to the same stimulus, the result is',
 ['habituation','sensitization','extinction','spontaneous recovery'],
 'A response that drops with repetition is habituation.');
Q('m4-types','r','When a response returns or increases with repeated exposure, the result is',
 ['sensitization','habituation','acquisition','extinction'],
 'A response that grows with repetition is sensitization.');
Q('m4-types','a','When you first walk into a friend\'s apartment you notice its smell, but after an hour you don\'t notice it at all. This is',
 ['habituation','sensitization','extinction','retrieval failure'],
 'The response faded with continued exposure: habituation.');
Q('m4-types','a','A coworker\'s shrill laugh bothers you more each time you hear it. This is',
 ['sensitization','habituation','spontaneous recovery','positive punishment'],
 'A response that grows with repeated exposure is sensitization.');
Q('m4-types','c','How does habituation differ from extinction?',
 ['Habituation: a response fades to one repeated stimulus; extinction: a CR fades when the CS appears without the UCS','Habituation: a CR fades when the CS appears without the UCS; extinction: a response fades to one repeated stimulus','Habituation: a response grows with one repeated stimulus; extinction: a CR fades when the CS appears without the UCS','Habituation: a response fades to one repeated stimulus; extinction: a CR fades when the UCS appears without the CS'],
 'Habituation is non-associative (one stimulus). Extinction fades a learned CR when the CS appears without the UCS; the association is not erased.');
Q('m4-types','c','Habituation and sensitization are both forms of',
 ['non-associative learning','classical conditioning','operant conditioning','observational learning'],
 'Both come from a single repeated stimulus with no pairing.');
TF('m4-types','t','In habituation, the response increases with repeated exposure.', false,
 'False. It decreases. An increase is sensitization.');
TF('m4-types','t','Non-associative learning requires pairing two stimuli.', false,
 'False. It comes from a single repeated stimulus; pairing is classical conditioning.');

/* ---- m4-princ ---- */
Q('m4-princ','r','Which are the three general principles of learning?',
 ['Metacognition, transfer-appropriate processing, forgetting','Encoding, storing, and retrieving information in memory','Procedural, implicit, and non-associative learning','Metacognition, transfer-appropriate processing, and elaboration'],
 'The three principles: metacognition, transfer-appropriate processing, and forgetting.');
Q('m4-princ','r','Metacognition is the ability to',
 ['monitor, control, and evaluate your own learning and memory','perform better when the test matches the original study conditions','recall information from long-term memory without a retrieval cue','learn new behaviors by watching others perform them first'],
 'Metacognition is knowing what you know and what you don\'t.');
Q('m4-princ','a','After a practice quiz, a student realizes she understands operant conditioning but keeps confusing the amnesia types, so she studies the amnesia types more. She is using',
 ['metacognition','transfer-appropriate processing','elaboration','rehearsal'],
 'Judging what you know and don\'t, then adjusting, is metacognition.');
Q('m4-princ','r','Transfer-appropriate processing states that memory is superior when',
 ['the test taps the same processes as the original encoding','the information is rehearsed aloud many times before a test','the learner monitors what they do and don\'t know well','the information is spaced across smaller units of time'],
 'Matching the test\'s processes to the encoding processes improves memory.');
Q('m4-princ','a','Students often sit in the same seats on exam day that they used during lectures. This habit reflects',
 ['transfer-appropriate processing','non-associative learning','retroactive interference','motivated forgetting'],
 'Matching test conditions to learning conditions is transfer-appropriate processing.');
Q('m4-princ','c','How does metacognition differ from transfer-appropriate processing?',
 ['Metacognition is knowing what you know; transfer-appropriate processing is matching test and study processes','Metacognition is matching test and study processes; transfer-appropriate processing is knowing what you know','Metacognition is learning without intent; transfer-appropriate processing is knowing what you know','Metacognition is knowing what you know; transfer-appropriate processing is learning without intent'],
 'Metacognition is self-monitoring; transfer-appropriate processing is about match between encoding and test.');
Q('m4-princ','r','As a principle of learning, forgetting is described as',
 ['not the enemy of learning','the opposite of metacognition','a failure that mostly comes with age','a sign of weak encoding skills'],
 'Forgetting is presented as a normal part of learning, not its enemy.');
Q('m4-princ','r','Forgetting is defined as',
 ['failure to recall previously learned information','failure to attend to an incoming sensory stimulus','failure to form an association between CS and UCS','failure to imitate the behavior of a social model'],
 'Forgetting is failing to recall what was learned.');
TF('m4-princ','t','Transfer-appropriate processing says memory is best when the test uses different processes than the original learning.', false,
 'False. Memory is best when the test taps the same processes.');

/* ---- m4-mem ---- */
Q('m4-mem','r','Memory is defined as',
 ['encoding, storing, and retrieving information','a relatively permanent change in behavior','monitoring and evaluating one\'s learning','learning by observing others'],
 'Memory covers encoding, storing, and retrieving. A permanent change in behavior is the definition of learning.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Working memory is',
 ['short-term: holding information briefly while using it','remembering the salient events of one\'s own life','general knowledge of world facts, words, and concepts','a long-term store that does not decay with age'],
 'Working memory is short-term and active.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Episodic memory is',
 ['remembering the salient events of one\'s life','general knowledge of world facts','holding information briefly while using it','knowing how to perform a skill'],
 'Episodic memory is for your own life events. General facts are semantic.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Semantic memory is',
 ['general knowledge of world facts, words, and numbers','remembering the salient events of one\'s own life','holding information briefly while working with it','long-term memory that needs no conscious thought'],
 'Semantic memory is general knowledge. Life events are episodic.');
Q('m4-mem','a','A student holds a phone number in mind just long enough to dial it. She is using',
 ['working memory','episodic memory','semantic memory','procedural memory'],
 'Holding information briefly while using it is working memory.');
Q('m4-mem','a','Recalling the day you got your acceptance letter, who you were with and how you felt, draws on',
 ['episodic memory','semantic memory','working memory','implicit memory'],
 'A salient event of your own life is episodic memory.');
Q('m4-mem','a','Knowing that a triangle has three sides, without remembering when you learned it, draws on',
 ['semantic memory','episodic memory','working memory','sensory memory'],
 'General knowledge of facts and concepts is semantic memory.');
Q('m4-mem','c','How does episodic memory differ from semantic memory?',
 ['Episodic is your own life events; semantic is general knowledge','Episodic is general knowledge; semantic is your own life events','Episodic is your own life events; semantic is short-term memory','Episodic is short-term memory; semantic is general knowledge'],
 'Episodic: personal events. Semantic: facts, words, concepts, numbers.');
Q('m4-mem','r','The Information Processing Model (IPM) explains',
 ['how raw information enters memory and is recalled when needed','why forgetting happens at an exponential rate after learning','how salient social models shape the behavior of learners','how consequences change the rate of the response they follow'],
 'The IPM traces information from entry to recall.');
Q('m4-mem','r','The three processes involved in remembering an episode, in order, are',
 ['encoding, storing, retrieving','storing, encoding, retrieving','encoding, retrieving, storing','retrieving, encoding, storing'],
 'Encode, then store, then retrieve. Attention, retention, motivation are parts of observational learning.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Encoding is',
 ['perceiving information and relating it to past knowledge','maintaining information in memory over a period of time','accessing stored information at the moment it is needed','repeating information aloud until it can be recalled'],
 'Encoding is perceiving and linking to what you already know.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Storing is',
 ['maintaining information over time','perceiving and relating information to past knowledge','accessing information when needed','forming mental images'],
 'Storing keeps information over time.');
Q('m4-mem','r','Retrieving is',
 ['accessing the information when it is needed','maintaining the information over time','relating information to past knowledge','monitoring what you don\'t know'],
 'Retrieving is pulling the information back out.');
Q('m4-mem','a','On an exam you recognize the question and pull the answer back out of memory. That step is',
 ['retrieving','encoding','storing','rehearsal'],
 'Accessing information when needed is retrieval.');
Q('m4-mem','a','While reading about place cells, a student connects them to a GPS she already understands. She is',
 ['encoding','storing','retrieving','habituating'],
 'Perceiving new information and relating it to past knowledge is encoding.');
Q('m4-mem','r','A failure at any of the three stages of memory can lead to',
 ['forgetting or false memories','extinction or spontaneous recovery','habituation or sensitization','implicit or procedural memories'],
 'Failures at encoding, storing, or retrieving lead to forgetting or false memories.');
Q('m4-mem','f','In the information processing model, which box is labeled B?',
 ['Working memory','Sensory memory','Long-term memory','Episodic memory'],
 'B sits between attention and long-term memory and has a rehearsal loop: working memory.', {fig:'f_ipm'});
Q('m4-mem','f','In the information processing model, what is arrow D, which moves information from the first store into the second?',
 ['Attention','Encoding','Retrieval','Rehearsal'],
 'Attention moves information from sensory memory into working memory.', {fig:'f_ipm'});
Q('m4-mem','f','In the information processing model, what is arrow E, which moves information from working memory into long-term memory?',
 ['Encode','Retrieve','Attention','Rehearsal'],
 'E is encoding into long-term memory; F, the opposite arrow, is retrieval.', {fig:'f_ipm'});
Q('m4-mem','f','In the information processing model, what is arrow F, which brings information from long-term memory back into working memory?',
 ['Retrieve','Encode','Attention','Forgetting'],
 'F is retrieval, from long-term memory back to working memory.', {fig:'f_ipm'});
Q('m4-mem','f','In the information processing model, label G marks the loop above working memory. G is',
 ['maintenance and elaborative rehearsal','attention and encoding into storage','storage and retrieval from long-term','decay and interference with old traces'],
 'G is rehearsal, which keeps information active in working memory.', {fig:'f_ipm'});
TF('m4-mem','t','Semantic memory refers to remembering the salient events of one\'s life.', false,
 'False. That is episodic memory. Semantic memory is general knowledge of facts.');

/* ---- m4-strat ---- */
Q('m4-strat','r','Rehearsal as a memory strategy means',
 ['rereading, repetition, or reading aloud','relating new information to what you know','forming mental images or mind maps','explaining information in your own words'],
 'Rehearsal is repetition: rereading, repeating, reading aloud.');
Q('m4-strat','r','Elaboration as a memory strategy means',
 ['relating new information to what you already know','repeating the information aloud until it sticks','using acronyms or silly sentences to recall steps','writing and answering your own practice questions'],
 'Elaboration links the new to the known.');
Q('m4-strat','r','Visualization as a memory strategy means',
 ['forming mental images or mind maps','repeating the material aloud','explaining the material to others','writing practice questions'],
 'Visualization is mental imagery and mind mapping.');
Q('m4-strat','r','Mnemonics as a memory strategy means',
 ['using acronyms or silly sentences to recall steps','forming mental images or mind maps of the material','relating new information to old knowledge you have','rereading the material several times in a row'],
 'Mnemonics use acronyms and silly sentences.');
Q('m4-strat','a','To remember the four parts of observational learning, a student makes up the sentence "All Rats Prefer Mozzarella." She is using',
 ['mnemonics','elaboration','visualization','rehearsal'],
 'A silly sentence built from first letters is a mnemonic.');
Q('m4-strat','a','A student studying the eye pictures the lens as a camera lens she has used and links each structure to a camera part. She is mainly using',
 ['elaboration','rehearsal','mnemonics','testing yourself'],
 'Relating new material to something already known is elaboration.');
Q('m4-strat','a','The night before the exam, a student reads her cheat sheet aloud five times in a row. She is using',
 ['rehearsal','elaboration','teaching','visualization'],
 'Repetition and reading aloud are rehearsal.');
Q('m4-strat','a','A student explains classical conditioning to her roommate in her own words. She is using',
 ['teaching','rehearsal','mnemonics','visualization'],
 'Explaining material to self or others in your own words is teaching.');
Q('m4-strat','a','A student writes practice questions on each lecture and answers them a day later to see what she remembers. She is using',
 ['testing yourself','teaching','rehearsal','associations'],
 'Writing and answering questions to check knowledge is testing yourself.');
Q('m4-strat','a','A student draws a single diagram linking rods, cones, the fovea, and the blind spot to one another. She is mainly using',
 ['associations','rehearsal','mnemonics','teaching'],
 'Creating links among the pieces that need remembering is associations.');
Q('m4-strat','c','How does elaboration differ from associations?',
 ['Elaboration links new information to prior knowledge; associations link new pieces to each other','Elaboration links new pieces to each other; associations link new information to prior knowledge','Elaboration forms mental images of new material; associations link new pieces to each other','Elaboration links new information to prior knowledge; associations rely on rereading it aloud'],
 'Elaboration ties to what you already know; associations tie the new items together.');
Q('m4-strat','f','The "15 ways to improve memory" infographic lists acronyms and silly sentences. These belong to which strategy?',
 ['Mnemonics','Elaboration','Visualization','Rehearsal'],
 'Acronyms and silly sentences are mnemonics.', {fig:'o_ways'});
Q('m4-strat','f','The infographic includes "Teach it to someone else." Which strategy is this?',
 ['Teaching','Elaboration','Associations','Testing yourself'],
 'Explaining material to others in your own words is teaching.', {fig:'o_ways'});
TF('m4-strat','t','Elaboration means repeating information aloud until it sticks.', false,
 'False. That is rehearsal. Elaboration relates new information to what you already know.');

/* ---- m4-cc ---- */
Q('m4-cc','r','Learning is defined as',
 ['a relatively permanent change in behavior from experience','a relatively permanent change in behavior from maturation','a temporary change in behavior from experience','a temporary change in behavior from maturation'],
 'Learning is a relatively permanent change in behavior from experience.');
Q('m4-cc','r','In classical conditioning, learning takes place when',
 ['a neutral stimulus is paired with one that already causes a response','a response is followed by a reward or a punishment that changes its rate','a learner watches a higher-status model perform the target behavior','a single stimulus is repeated many times until the response changes'],
 'Pairing a neutral stimulus with a UCS is classical conditioning.');
Q('m4-cc','a','Each time a cat hears the can opener, food follows. Soon the cat begins to salivate at the sound of the can opener alone. This is',
 ['classical conditioning','operant conditioning','habituation','observational learning'],
 'A neutral sound paired with food until it triggers a response on its own is classical conditioning.');
Q('m4-cc','a','In Pavlov-style conditioning, food makes a dog salivate before any training. The food is the',
 ['unconditioned stimulus','conditioned stimulus','neutral stimulus','conditioned response'],
 'Food produces salivation without learning: the unconditioned stimulus.');
Q('m4-cc','a','After conditioning, a dog salivates when it hears a whistle alone. The salivation to the whistle is the',
 ['conditioned response','unconditioned response','conditioned stimulus','neutral stimulus'],
 'A learned response to the CS is the conditioned response.');
Q('m4-cc','a','After conditioning, the whistle that now triggers salivation is the',
 ['conditioned stimulus','unconditioned stimulus','neutral stimulus','conditioned response'],
 'The once-neutral stimulus that now triggers the response is the CS.');
Q('m4-cc','r','The stage during which an organism learns to associate the CS with the UCS is called',
 ['acquisition','extinction','spontaneous recovery','shaping'],
 'Acquisition is the learning stage. Extinction is when the CR is eliminated.');
Q('m4-cc','r','In classical conditioning, each pairing of the stimuli is called a',
 ['trial','reinforcer','conditioned stimulus','conditioned response'],
 'Each pairing is a trial.');
Q('m4-cc','r','Repeated trials strengthen, or reinforce,',
 ['the association between the CS and UCS','the time gap between the CS and UCS','the unconditioned response to the UCS','the extinction of the conditioned response'],
 'Trials reinforce the CS–UCS association.');
Q('m4-cc','r','Extinction in classical conditioning is the process by which',
 ['the CR fades as the CS is repeatedly presented without the UCS','the CR reappears some time after it was thought to be eliminated','the CS is paired with the UCS for the first time during a trial','a response decreases with repeated exposure to a single stimulus'],
 'Extinction: CS without UCS, repeatedly, until the CR fades.');
Q('m4-cc','r','Spontaneous recovery is',
 ['the reappearance of a conditioned response after extinction','the first appearance of a conditioned response during acquisition','the gradual loss of a conditioned response over repeated trials','a response that grows stronger with repeated exposure to a stimulus'],
 'After extinction, the CR can spontaneously come back.');
Q('m4-cc','a','A dog conditioned to salivate to a bell hears the bell many times with no food, and the salivation stops. A week later the bell rings and the dog salivates again. The return of salivation is',
 ['spontaneous recovery','acquisition','sensitization','reinforcement'],
 'The CR reappearing after extinction is spontaneous recovery.');
Q('m4-cc','a','A dog conditioned to salivate to a bell hears the bell many times with no food, and gradually stops salivating. This is',
 ['extinction','habituation','negative punishment','retrieval failure'],
 'Presenting the CS without the UCS until the CR disappears is extinction.');
Q('m4-cc','r','After extinction, if the CS and UCS are paired again, the association is relearned',
 ['more quickly than the first time','more slowly than the first time','at about the same rate','only after a long delay'],
 'Extinction does not erase the response; relearning is faster.');
Q('m4-cc','r','Classical conditioning works best on',
 ['automatic processes such as salivation','complex skills such as playing an instrument','voluntary behaviors that are rewarded','attitudes, values, and beliefs'],
 'It works well on automatic processes and is less effective for complex learning; it is passive learning.');
Q('m4-cc','c','Which statement about extinction in classical conditioning is accurate?',
 ['The response is not totally lost','The UCR fades along with the CR','The CS is paired more often with the UCS','Relearning takes longer than the first time'],
 'Extinction does not mean the response is totally lost; it can recover and is relearned faster.');
Q('m4-cc','f','In the conditioning figure, panel 1 shows food producing salivation. What are the hidden labels A and B?',
 ['Unconditioned stimulus; unconditioned response','Conditioned stimulus; conditioned response','Neutral stimulus; unconditioned response','Neutral stimulus; conditioned response'],
 'Before conditioning, food is the UCS and salivation to it is the UCR.', {fig:'f_pavlov'});
Q('m4-cc','f','In the conditioning figure, panel 2 shows the whistle producing no salivation. What is label C?',
 ['Neutral stimulus','Conditioned stimulus','Unconditioned stimulus','Conditioned response'],
 'Before pairing, the whistle is a neutral stimulus.', {fig:'f_pavlov'});
Q('m4-cc','f','In the conditioning figure, panel 4 shows the whistle alone producing salivation. What are the hidden labels D and E?',
 ['Conditioned stimulus; conditioned response','Neutral stimulus; unconditioned response','Unconditioned stimulus; conditioned response','Conditioned stimulus; unconditioned response'],
 'After conditioning, the whistle is the CS and salivation to it is the CR.', {fig:'f_pavlov'});
Q('m4-cc','f','In the conditioning figure, panel 3 (whistle plus food) shows which stage of classical conditioning?',
 ['Acquisition','Extinction','Spontaneous recovery','Shaping'],
 'Pairing the CS with the UCS across trials is acquisition.', {fig:'f_pavlov'});
TF('m4-cc','t','Classical conditioning is a form of passive learning.', true,
 'True. The learner responds automatically; operant conditioning is the active one.');
TF('m4-cc','t','In extinction, the CS is repeatedly presented together with the UCS.', false,
 'False. In extinction the CS is presented without the UCS.');
TF('m4-cc','t','Classical conditioning is highly effective for complex forms of learning.', false,
 'False. It works well on automatic processes but is less effective for complex learning.');

/* ---- m4-oc ---- */
Q('m4-oc','r','Operant conditioning changes the rate of a response on the basis of',
 ['the consequences that follow the response','a stimulus that comes before the response','repeated exposure to a single stimulus','watching a model perform the response'],
 'Operant conditioning is driven by consequences.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Conditioning is called "operant" because the organism must',
 ['operate on its environment to get a reward','be operated on by a preceding stimulus','operate a lever inside a laboratory box','respond to a stimulus it cannot control'],
 'The organism has to act on its environment to get the reward.');
Q('m4-oc','r','The Law of Effect states that behavior is',
 ['strengthened by positive consequences and weakened by negative ones','strengthened mainly by repetition and practice over many trials','weakened each time it is paired with a previously neutral stimulus','learned mainly by watching models of higher status perform it first'],
 'Positive consequences strengthen behavior; negative ones weaken it.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Reinforcement is a process that',
 ['increases the rate or probability of the response it follows','strengthens a reflex to a stimulus that comes before it','pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus','removes a conditioned response through repeated trials'],
 'Reinforcement always increases behavior; punishment decreases it.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Punishment is a stimulus that',
 ['decreases the rate of the response it follows','weakens the link between a CS and a UCS','precedes and triggers a reflexive response','pairs with a neutral stimulus over trials'],
 'Punishment decreases the response that it follows.');
Q('m4-oc','r','In the terms positive/negative reinforcement and positive/negative punishment, "positive" and "negative" refer to whether a stimulus is',
 ['added or removed','pleasant or unpleasant','learned or unlearned','immediate or delayed'],
 'Positive = adding a stimulus; negative = removing one.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A parent gives a child a cookie every time the child cleans their room, and the cleaning becomes more frequent. This is',
 ['positive reinforcement','negative reinforcement','positive punishment','negative punishment'],
 'Adding a stimulus to increase behavior is positive reinforcement.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A manager stops nagging an employee once the reports come in on time, and the employee keeps turning reports in on time. This is',
 ['negative reinforcement','positive reinforcement','negative punishment','positive punishment'],
 'Removing a stimulus (nagging) to increase a behavior is negative reinforcement.');
Q('m4-oc','a','Parents ground a teen for missing curfew, and the teen misses curfew less often. This is',
 ['positive punishment','negative punishment','negative reinforcement','positive reinforcement'],
 'Adding a stimulus (grounding) to decrease a behavior is positive punishment.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A dog paws at its owner for attention, and the owner withholds petting until the pawing stops. The pawing becomes less frequent. This is',
 ['negative punishment','positive punishment','negative reinforcement','positive reinforcement'],
 'Removing a pleasant stimulus (petting) to decrease a behavior is negative punishment.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A driver buckles her seatbelt to make the car\'s beeping stop, and she starts buckling up right away every time. The beep stopping is',
 ['negative reinforcement','positive punishment','negative punishment','positive reinforcement'],
 'Removing an annoying stimulus to increase a behavior is negative reinforcement.');
Q('m4-oc','c','Negative reinforcement differs from punishment because negative reinforcement',
 ['increases a behavior','decreases a behavior','removes a stimulus','adds a stimulus'],
 'All reinforcement increases behavior; punishment decreases it. Removing a stimulus does not separate them, since negative punishment also removes one.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Shaping is the process of',
 ['reinforcing closer and closer approximations of the desired behavior','pairing a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus over trials','removing a pleasant stimulus to decrease an unwanted behavior','withholding a reinforcer until the response gradually stops'],
 'Shaping reinforces successive approximations of the target behavior.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A therapist teaches an adult with a cognitive delay to brush his teeth by rewarding him first for picking up the brush, then for adding toothpaste, then for brushing. This is',
 ['shaping','acquisition','observational learning','spontaneous recovery'],
 'Reinforcing closer and closer approximations step by step is shaping.');
Q('m4-oc','r','A primary reinforcer is a stimulus that',
 ['increases a response without previous learning','increases a response because of a learned association','decreases a response when removed','is paired with a neutral stimulus'],
 'Primary reinforcers need no learning. Learned ones are secondary.');
Q('m4-oc','r','A secondary reinforcer is a stimulus that',
 ['increases a response because of a learned association','increases a response without previous learning','decreases a response when added','triggers a reflex without training'],
 'A secondary (conditioned) reinforcer works through a learned association.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A paycheck keeps an employee coming to work. Money in this case is a',
 ['secondary reinforcer','primary reinforcer','positive punishment','neutral stimulus'],
 'Money\'s value is learned, so it is a secondary (conditioned) reinforcer.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A hungry rat presses a lever more often after each press delivers a food pellet. The food is a',
 ['primary reinforcer','secondary reinforcer','conditioned stimulus','negative reinforcer'],
 'Food when hungry works without prior learning: a primary reinforcer.');
Q('m4-oc','r','For punishment to be effective, it should be delivered',
 ['immediately after the response','at the end of the day','once the behavior becomes severe','after a warning period'],
 'A lapse of time between response and punishment delays learning.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Which is listed as a limitation of punishment?',
 ['It can teach aggressive behavior','It works best when delivered immediately','It works best when applied consistently','It works best when it fits the crime'],
 'Limits: temporary suppression, can teach aggression, can create anxiety.');
Q('m4-oc','r','Which is a guideline for making punishment effective?',
 ['Introduce alternatives to the punished response','Vary the punishment so it stays unpredictable','Delay the punishment so the learner can reflect','Make the punishment stronger than the offense'],
 'Guidelines: immediate, consistent, offer alternatives, fit the crime; note it may suppress all behavior.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A child stops hitting his sibling after being scolded, but starts again a week later. Which limitation of punishment does this show?',
 ['Temporary suppression of behavior','Teaching of aggressive behavior','Anxiety in the learner','Suppression of all behavior'],
 'The behavior was only suppressed for a while, then returned.');
Q('m4-oc','c','Which pair of features describes operant conditioning, compared with classical conditioning?',
 ['Voluntary response; learner is active','Involuntary response; learner is passive','Voluntary response; learner is passive','Involuntary response; learner is active'],
 'Operant: voluntary and active. Classical: involuntary and passive.');
Q('m4-oc','c','In classical conditioning the stimulus comes before the response. In operant conditioning the key stimulus (the consequence) comes',
 ['after the response','before the response','during the response','before and after it'],
 'In operant conditioning the consequence follows the response.');
Q('m4-oc','c','Which is a similarity between classical and operant conditioning?',
 ['Both can become extinct and recover spontaneously','Both rely on voluntary responses from the learner','Both make the learner an active participant','Both depend on stimuli that follow the response'],
 'Both need acquisition and can show extinction and spontaneous recovery.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A student studies more after an A on a quiz. After a tone has been paired many times with a puff of air to the eye, a baby blinks at the tone alone. Which conditioning type matches each, in order?',
 ['Operant; classical','Classical; operant','Operant; operant','Classical; classical'],
 'Studying is voluntary behavior shaped by a consequence (operant). Blinking to the tone is a reflex now triggered by a paired stimulus (classical).');
Q('m4-oc','f','In the reinforcement grid, cell A is where a stimulus is added and behavior increases. A is',
 ['positive reinforcement','negative reinforcement','positive punishment','negative punishment'],
 'Added + increases = positive reinforcement.', {fig:'f_reinf'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the reinforcement grid, cell B is where a stimulus is added and behavior decreases. B is',
 ['positive punishment','negative punishment','positive reinforcement','negative reinforcement'],
 'Added + decreases = positive punishment.', {fig:'f_reinf'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the reinforcement grid, which cell is negative reinforcement?',
 ['C','A','B','D'],
 'Negative reinforcement is stimulus removed + behavior increases: cell C.', {fig:'f_reinf'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the reinforcement grid, cell D is where a stimulus is removed and behavior decreases. D is',
 ['negative punishment','negative reinforcement','positive punishment','extinction'],
 'Removed + decreases = negative punishment.', {fig:'f_reinf'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the comparison table, what goes in hidden cells A and B (nature of response, classical then operant)?',
 ['Involuntary; voluntary','Voluntary; involuntary','Involuntary; involuntary','Voluntary; voluntary'],
 'Classical responses are involuntary (reflexive); operant responses are voluntary.', {fig:'f_cvo'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the comparison table, what goes in hidden cells G and H (role of learner, classical then operant)?',
 ['Passive; active','Active; passive','Passive; passive','Active; active'],
 'The classical learner is passive; the operant learner is active.', {fig:'f_cvo'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the comparison table, what goes in hidden cells C and D (timing of stimulus, classical then operant)?',
 ['Precedes the response; after the response','After the response; precedes the response','Precedes the response; precedes the response','After the response; after the response'],
 'In classical conditioning the stimulus precedes the response; in operant it comes after.', {fig:'f_cvo'});
Q('m4-oc','f','In the comparison table, what goes in hidden cells E and F (timing of response, classical then operant)?',
 ['After the stimulus; before the stimulus','Before the stimulus; after the stimulus','After the stimulus; after the stimulus','Before the stimulus; before the stimulus'],
 'Classical responses come after the stimulus; operant responses come before the consequence.', {fig:'f_cvo'});
Q('m4-oc','f','The figure shows a dog with its tail tucked, weight shifted backward, and mouth closed and tense, placed beside the limitations of punishment. Which limitation does it illustrate?',
 ['Punishment can create anxiety in the learner','Punishment only suppresses behavior temporarily','Punishment must fit the crime','Punishment must be delivered immediately'],
 'The fearful body language illustrates that punishment can create anxiety.', {fig:'o_dog'});
TF('m4-oc','t','Negative reinforcement increases the behavior it follows, even though a stimulus is removed.', true,
 'True. All reinforcement increases behavior; negative only means a stimulus is taken away.');
TF('m4-oc','t','In operant conditioning the learner is passive.', false,
 'False. The operant learner is active; the classical learner is passive.');
TF('m4-oc','t','Punishment may suppress or decrease all behavior, not just the target behavior.', true,
 'True. That is one of the cautions about punishment.');
TF('m4-oc','t','Grounding a teen for missing curfew is an example of negative punishment.', false,
 'False. Grounding adds a stimulus, so it is positive punishment.');

/* ---- m4-obs ---- */
Q('m4-obs','r','Observational learning is',
 ['learning by watching the behavior of others','learning from the consequences of one\'s own behavior','learning by pairing two stimuli','learning without any intent'],
 'Observational learning comes from watching others.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A child learns how to play kickball by watching older kids at recess before ever joining in. This is',
 ['observational learning','operant conditioning','classical conditioning','sensitization'],
 'Learning by watching others is observational learning.');
Q('m4-obs','r','Social Learning Theory was proposed by',
 ['Bandura (1977)','Harlow (1958)','Ebbinghaus (1880s)','Luria (1960s)'],
 'Bandura\'s Social Learning Theory (1977).');
Q('m4-obs','r','Social Learning Theory states that people learn new behaviors by',
 ['observing the behavior of salient others','receiving reinforcement for each response','pairing neutral stimuli with reflexes','repeated exposure to a single stimulus'],
 'Learning by observing salient others is the core of Social Learning Theory.');
Q('m4-obs','r','In Social Learning Theory, social models are',
 ['salient others with higher status or authority than the learner','peers of equal status whom the learner happens to sit beside','strangers whose behavior the learner has never observed before','the learner\'s own past behaviors that were reinforced over time'],
 'Social models have higher status or authority: parents, older siblings, superheroes, mentors.');
Q('m4-obs','e','Which person is the best example of a social model?',
 ['An older sibling','A stranger on the bus','A younger classmate','A younger cousin'],
 'Social models have higher status or authority than the learner.');
Q('m4-obs','r','The four parts of observational learning, in order, are',
 ['attention, retention, initiation (production), motivation','retention, attention, initiation (production), motivation','attention, retention, motivation, initiation (production)','attention, initiation (production), retention, motivation'],
 'Bandura\'s four parts: attention, retention, initiation (production), motivation.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A medical student watches a resident place an IV, remembers the steps, and has the skill to do it, but sees no situation where doing it would be useful to her. Which part of observational learning is missing?',
 ['Motivation','Attention','Retention','Initiation (production)'],
 'She attended, retained, and can perform; without a situation that makes it useful, motivation is missing.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A trainee watches a procedure but is texting the whole time and misses the key steps. Which part of observational learning has failed?',
 ['Attention','Retention','Motivation','Initiation (production)'],
 'Without attention to the model, nothing can be learned from it.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A learner watched a demonstration closely and wants to repeat it, but a week later cannot recall the steps. Which part has failed?',
 ['Retention','Attention','Initiation (production)','Motivation'],
 'Holding the observed behavior in memory is retention.');
Q('m4-obs','c','Compared with classical and operant conditioning, observational learning',
 ['describes the vast majority of human learning','applies mainly to automatic reflexes','requires a consequence after each response','works mainly through primary reinforcers'],
 'Observational learning accounts for most human learning; conditioning examples in humans are harder to find.');
Q('m4-obs','e','Which is an example of observational learning?',
 ['An OSCE skill learned by "see one, do one"','Salivating at the sound of a can opener','Typing faster after hours of practice','No longer noticing a ticking clock'],
 'Watching then doing is observational learning; the others are conditioning, procedural learning, and habituation.');
Q('m4-obs','r','The three primary learning domains are',
 ['cognitive, psychomotor, and affective','cognitive, procedural, and implicit','episodic, semantic, and working','sensory, working, and long-term'],
 'Cognitive, psychomotor, and affective, which together cover KSAs.');
Q('m4-obs','r','The cognitive domain consists of',
 ['declarative knowledge, facts, concepts, and theories','physical skills, motor movements, procedures, and protocols','attitudes, values, beliefs, intentions, and emotions','reflexes, habits, and other automatic responses'],
 'Cognitive = declarative knowledge.');
Q('m4-obs','r','The psychomotor domain consists of',
 ['physical skills, procedures, and protocols','facts, concepts, theories, and knowledge','attitudes, values, beliefs, and intentions','motivations, emotions, and early experience'],
 'Psychomotor = physical skills, procedures, protocols.');
Q('m4-obs','r','The affective domain consists of',
 ['attitudes, values, beliefs, and emotions','facts, concepts, theories, and knowledge','fine and gross motor movements and skills','procedures, protocols, and physical skills'],
 'Affective = attitudes, values, beliefs, intentions, motivations, emotions.');
Q('m4-obs','c','Which learning domain is the hardest to teach and for students to be persuaded to learn?',
 ['Affective','Cognitive','Psychomotor','Procedural'],
 'Affective learning is hardest: attitudes and beliefs form early and are shaped by parenting.');
Q('m4-obs','c','Why is the cognitive domain the easiest to teach and learn?',
 ['People are innately motivated to be correct','Facts need less encoding than physical skills','Cognitive learning relies on watching models','Declarative knowledge decays more slowly'],
 'Humans are motivated to be correct and dislike being misinformed.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A nursing student practices performing the hand-hygiene protocol until she can do every step correctly. This falls in the',
 ['psychomotor domain','cognitive domain','affective domain','semantic domain'],
 'Procedures and protocols belong to the psychomotor domain.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A public health campaign tries to change people\'s beliefs about vaccines. It is targeting the',
 ['affective domain','cognitive domain','psychomotor domain','procedural domain'],
 'Beliefs and attitudes are the affective domain.');
Q('m4-obs','f','In the observational learning model, what is the hidden first step, A?',
 ['Attention','Retention','Motivation','Encoding'],
 'Step A ("focuses on a model\'s behaviour") is attention.', {fig:'f_obs'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the observational learning model, step C says the observer has the ability to perform the behavior. What is C?',
 ['Production (initiation)','Retention','Motivation','Attention'],
 'C is production (initiation): being able to execute the behavior.', {fig:'f_obs'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the observational learning model, step B says the observer keeps the behavior in memory. What is B?',
 ['Retention','Attention','Production (initiation)','Motivation'],
 'B is retention.', {fig:'f_obs'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the observational learning model, the last step, D, is a situation in which the behavior becomes useful. What is D?',
 ['Motivation','Production (initiation)','Retention','Attention'],
 'D is motivation.', {fig:'f_obs'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the learning-domains figure, which domain is at A, the "harder to teach and learn" end of the curve?',
 ['Affective','Cognitive','Psychomotor','Procedural'],
 'The affective domain is hardest; cognitive is at the easy end.', {fig:'f_domains'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the learning-domains figure, which domain is at C, the "easier to teach and learn" end?',
 ['Cognitive','Affective','Psychomotor','Semantic'],
 'The cognitive domain is easiest because people are motivated to be correct.', {fig:'f_domains'});
Q('m4-obs','f','In the learning-domains figure, what is B, the domain in the middle of the curve?',
 ['Psychomotor','Cognitive','Affective','Procedural'],
 'Psychomotor sits between cognitive (easiest) and affective (hardest).', {fig:'f_domains'});
TF('m4-obs','t','Social models have lower status than the learner.', false,
 'False. They have higher status or authority, such as parents or mentors.');
TF('m4-obs','t','Observational learning describes the vast majority of human learning.', true,
 'True. Most knowledge, skills, and attitudes are acquired this way.');
TF('m4-obs','t','Attitudes and beliefs belong to the cognitive domain.', false,
 'False. They belong to the affective domain.');

/* ---- m4-forget ---- */
Q('m4-forget','r','Which list gives the five causes of normal forgetting?',
 ['Decay, interference, retrieval failure, motivated forgetting, organic factors','Decay, interference, encoding error, storage error, extinction','Retrieval failure, motivated forgetting, encoding error, habituation, spontaneous recovery','Organic factors, storage error, extinction, habituation, spontaneous recovery'],
 'Normal forgetting: decay, interference, retrieval failure, motivated forgetting, organic factors.');
Q('m4-forget','r','With normal aging, which kind of memory tends to decay?',
 ['Declarative memories','Procedural memory','Implicit memories','Motor skill memories'],
 'Declarative memories tend to decay with age; procedural memory does not.');
Q('m4-forget','c','An older adult struggles to recall names and dates but can still ride a bike and knit without trouble. This pattern fits the idea that',
 ['declarative memories decay with age but procedural memory does not','declarative memories improve with age while procedural memory fades','both kinds of memory decay at about the same rate with age','neither kind of memory is affected by normal aging'],
 'Declarative memories decay with age; procedural memory holds up.');
Q('m4-forget','e','Which ability is least likely to decline with normal aging?',
 ['Riding a bike learned in childhood','Recalling the names of new coworkers','Recalling the dates of recent events','Recalling facts learned last month'],
 'Procedural memory does not decay with age.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Decay of the memory trace means that',
 ['memories fade or deteriorate over time','new memories block old ones','memories are present but out of reach','memories are pushed away on purpose'],
 'Decay is fading over time; it usually affects short-term memory.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Decay of the memory trace usually results in the loss of',
 ['short-term memory','long-term memory','procedural memory','implicit memories'],
 'Decay usually costs short-term memory; long-term memory is more permanent.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Interfering material means memory is determined by',
 ['what we do before or after learning','how long ago the original event occurred','whether we want to remember the material','damage to the brain from illness or injury'],
 'Interference comes from material learned before or after.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Retroactive interference occurs when',
 ['a later event interferes with an earlier one','an earlier event interferes with a later one','a fading trace interferes with recall','an injury interferes with old memories'],
 'Retroactive = backward: new interferes with old.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Proactive interference occurs when',
 ['a previous event interferes with learning a later one','a later event interferes with memory of an earlier one','a missing cue interferes with recalling a stored memory','brain damage interferes with forming new memories'],
 'Proactive = forward: old interferes with new.');
Q('m4-forget','a','After learning Spanish, a student finds that Spanish words keep slipping in when she tries to learn Italian. This is',
 ['proactive interference','retroactive interference','retrieval failure','decay of memory trace'],
 'Old learning (Spanish) interfering with new learning (Italian) is proactive.');
Q('m4-forget','a','After you change your phone passcode, you can no longer remember the old one. The new passcode is causing',
 ['retroactive interference','proactive interference','anterograde amnesia','motivated forgetting'],
 'New learning blocking an older memory is retroactive interference.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Retrieval failure means that',
 ['the memory is present but out of reach without a cue','the memory has faded away through lack of use','the memory was never encoded in the first place','the memory has been pushed out of mind on purpose'],
 'With retrieval failure the memory exists but needs a cue.');
Q('m4-forget','a','You know the actor\'s name, it is "on the tip of your tongue," and it comes back as soon as a friend says the first letter. This is',
 ['retrieval failure','decay of memory trace','proactive interference','motivated forgetting'],
 'The memory was present but out of reach until a cue: retrieval failure.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Motivated forgetting means',
 ['we forget long-term memories we do not want to keep','we forget because newer material interferes with old','we forget because the memory trace fades over time','we forget because of illness or injury to the brain'],
 'Motivated forgetting is purposeful suppression.');
Q('m4-forget','a','A person deliberately avoids thinking about an embarrassing moment until she truly can\'t recall the details. This is',
 ['motivated forgetting','retrieval failure','retroactive interference','decay of memory trace'],
 'Purposely suppressing a memory is motivated forgetting.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Organic factors in forgetting refer to',
 ['biological pathology, illness, or accidents','newer information interfering with older','a lack of cues at the moment of retrieval','the natural fading of short-term memory'],
 'Organic factors are biological causes such as illness or injury.');
Q('m4-forget','r','The three types of amnesia listed under organic factors are',
 ['disease/trauma, retrograde, and anterograde','retroactive, proactive, and motivated','decay, interference, and retrieval failure','episodic, semantic, and working'],
 'Organic amnesia: disease/trauma, retrograde, anterograde.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Retrograde amnesia is',
 ['loss of memory from before an event or accident','inability to process new information after damage','forgetting caused by later learning','forgetting caused by earlier learning'],
 'Retrograde = lose the past.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Anterograde amnesia is',
 ['inability to process new information after the damage','loss of memories formed before the damage occurred','forgetting caused by deliberate suppression of memories','memory loss caused only by decreased blood circulation'],
 'Anterograde = cannot form new memories after the damage.');
Q('m4-forget','a','After a car accident, a man cannot remember his own name or address but can learn and remember new information. This is',
 ['retrograde amnesia','anterograde amnesia','proactive interference','retrieval failure'],
 'Losing memories from before the event is retrograde amnesia.');
Q('m4-forget','a','After surgery that removed her hippocampus, a woman remembers her childhood but cannot remember people she met yesterday. This is',
 ['anterograde amnesia','retrograde amnesia','retroactive interference','decay of memory trace'],
 'Old memories intact, no new ones: anterograde amnesia.');
Q('m4-forget','c','How does retrograde amnesia differ from anterograde amnesia?',
 ['Retrograde loses memories from before the damage; anterograde blocks new ones after it','Retrograde blocks new memories after the damage; anterograde loses memories from before it','Retrograde loses memories from before the damage; anterograde comes from deliberate suppression','Retrograde comes from deliberate suppression; anterograde blocks new ones after it'],
 'Retrograde: past lost. Anterograde: future blocked.');
Q('m4-forget','r','Which is an example of the disease/trauma type of organic amnesia?',
 ['Memory deficit from Alzheimer\'s disease','Forgetting an old passcode after learning a new one','Blocking out an embarrassing memory','Losing a name on the tip of the tongue'],
 'Disease/trauma includes brain-cell deterioration, poor circulation, stroke, Alzheimer\'s.');
Q('m4-forget','c','Retroactive interference and retrograde amnesia both involve losing older memories. What separates them?',
 ['Interference comes from newer learning; retrograde amnesia comes from organic damage','Interference comes from organic damage; retrograde amnesia comes from newer learning','Interference affects procedural memory; retrograde amnesia affects declarative memories','Interference comes from newer learning; retrograde amnesia comes from deliberate suppression'],
 'Retroactive interference is newer learning getting in the way; retrograde amnesia is caused by brain damage or illness.');
Q('m4-forget','f','In the "Five Impediments to Remembering" box, item C (hidden) is described as "we lack sufficient reminders." It matches which cause of normal forgetting?',
 ['Retrieval failure','Decay of memory trace','Motivated forgetting','Organic factors'],
 'Lacking retrieval cues is retrieval failure.', {fig:'f_impede'});
Q('m4-forget','f','In the "Five Impediments to Remembering" box, item E (hidden) is "we deliberately attempt to keep things out of mind." It matches',
 ['motivated forgetting','retrieval failure','interfering material','decay of memory trace'],
 'Deliberate suppression is motivated forgetting.', {fig:'f_impede'});
Q('m4-forget','f','In the "Five Impediments to Remembering" box, item D (hidden) is "other memories get in the way." It matches',
 ['interfering material','decay of memory trace','retrieval failure','motivated forgetting'],
 'Other memories getting in the way is interference.', {fig:'f_impede'});
Q('m4-forget','f','In the "Five Impediments to Remembering" box, item B (hidden) is "memories fade over time." It matches',
 ['decay of memory trace','interfering material','retrieval failure','organic factors'],
 'Fading over time is decay.', {fig:'f_impede'});
Q('m4-forget','f','In the "Five Impediments to Remembering" box, item A (hidden) is "we don\'t learn the information in the first place." Which stage of memory failed?',
 ['Encoding','Storing','Retrieving','Rehearsal'],
 'Never learning it in the first place is a failure of encoding.', {fig:'f_impede'});
TF('m4-forget','t','Proactive interference is when a later event interferes with an earlier one.', false,
 'False. That is retroactive. Proactive is an earlier event interfering with a later one.');
TF('m4-forget','t','Retrieval failure can be resolved with a retrieval cue.', true,
 'True. The memory is still there and a cue brings it back.');
TF('m4-forget','t','Anterograde amnesia leaves memories formed before the damage intact.', true,
 'True. The problem is processing new information after the damage.');

/* ---- m4-adapt ---- */
Q('m4-adapt','s','Shereshevsky ("S"), the mnemonist studied by Luria, never forgot anything. What effect did this have on his life?',
 ['He struggled to function, distracted by a flood of details','He excelled at work because he could recall details easily','He handled everyday tasks with little trouble','He functioned well once he learned mnemonics'],
 'His limitless memory was debilitating: constant details and associations distracted him.');
Q('m4-adapt','s','What does the case of Shereshevsky suggest about forgetting?',
 ['Forgetting is adaptive and makes the mind more efficient','Forgetting is usually a sign of disease or brain injury','Forgetting can be prevented with strong mnemonics','Forgetting is the main enemy of efficient learning'],
 'Remembering everything is overwhelming; forgetting is adaptive.');
Q('m4-adapt','r','Which is given as a reason forgetting is adaptive?',
 ['Our brains need room for new information','Forgetting makes older memories stronger','Forgetting removes the need for retrieval cues','Forgetting keeps procedural memories from decaying'],
 'Reasons: remembering everything overwhelms, is inefficient, and leaves no room for new information.');
Q('m4-adapt','s','Ebbinghaus found that we forget information',
 ['at an exponential rate, fastest right after learning','at a steady, even rate across the whole first week','slowly at first, then quickly near the week\'s end','mostly after several days have passed since learning'],
 'Forgetting is exponential: the biggest loss comes first.');
Q('m4-adapt','s','On the forgetting curve, about how much new information is forgotten within the first 24 hours?',
 ['70%','30%','50%','90%'],
 'About 70% is forgotten in 24 hours and about 90% within a week.');
Q('m4-adapt','s','On the forgetting curve, about how much of what we learned is forgotten after a week?',
 ['90%','70%','50%','30%'],
 'About 90% is forgotten within a week.');
Q('m4-adapt','f','On Ebbinghaus\'s forgetting curve, when does memory retention drop most steeply?',
 ['Right after training','Between days 4 and 5','At the end of the week','Evenly across all days'],
 'The curve plunges right after learning, then levels off.', {fig:'o_ebb'});
Q('m4-adapt','r','Which strategy is recommended to decrease the rate of forgetting?',
 ['Space learning across smaller units of time','Study everything in one long, unbroken session','Move information out of working memory quickly','Wait about a week before reviewing new material'],
 'Spacing learning, using memory strategies, and interrupting study for primacy and recency effects slow forgetting.');
TF('m4-adapt','t','Ebbinghaus found that most forgetting happens long after learning, not right away.', false,
 'False. Forgetting is exponential; the steepest loss is right after learning.');
TF('m4-adapt','t','Forgetting aligns with and supports the information processing model of memory.', true,
 'True. That is one of the listed reasons forgetting is adaptive.');

/* ---- added for coverage ---- */
Q('m4-types','c','How does implicit learning differ from procedural learning?',
 ['Implicit learning happens without intent and is hard to express','Implicit learning builds skills like cooking and biking','Implicit learning requires a reinforcer after each response','Implicit learning comes only from watching a social model'],
 'Implicit learning is unintended and hard to put into words; procedural learning is about skills like cooking and biking.');
Q('m4-types','r','Implicit learning leads to',
 ['implicit memories','episodic memories','semantic memories','working memory'],
 'Implicit learning produces implicit memories: long-term, encoded without conscious thought.');
Q('m4-mem','c','Which term covers encoding, storing, and retrieving information as a whole?',
 ['Memory','Learning','Metacognition','Perception'],
 'Memory is encoding, storing, and retrieving. Learning is a lasting change in behavior from experience.');
Q('m4-strat','a','To learn the ear\'s structures, a student closes her eyes and pictures sound traveling down the canal and into the spiral cochlea. She is using',
 ['visualization','rehearsal','mnemonics','testing yourself'],
 'Forming mental images is visualization.');
Q('m4-strat','c','How does teaching differ from testing yourself as a memory strategy?',
 ['Teaching: explaining in your own words; testing yourself: answering your own questions','Teaching: answering your own questions; testing yourself: explaining in your own words','Teaching: explaining in your own words; testing yourself: rereading aloud','Teaching: forming mental images; testing yourself: answering your own questions'],
 'Teaching: explain in your own words. Testing yourself: write and answer questions.');
TF('m4-strat','t','Creating associations among the pieces of information you need to remember is a strategy for improving memory.', true,
 'True. Associations link the items that need to be remembered.');
Q('m4-cc','c','Which change counts as learning?',
 ['A lasting change in behavior caused by experience','A brief change in behavior caused by fatigue','A change in behavior caused by growth alone','A reflex that is present from birth'],
 'Learning is a relatively permanent change in behavior that results from experience.');
Q('m4-cc','r','In classical conditioning, each trial serves to',
 ['reinforce the association between the CS and UCS','weaken the unconditioned response to the UCS','replace the UCS with a new neutral stimulus','turn the conditioned response into a UCR'],
 'Trials strengthen, or reinforce, the CS–UCS association.');
TF('m4-cc','t','In classical conditioning, repeated trials reinforce the association between the CS and the UCS.', true,
 'True. Each pairing strengthens the association.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A rat presses a lever and gets food, so it presses more; when pressing brings a shock, it presses less. Which principle summarizes both results?',
 ['Law of Effect','Shaping','Reinforcement','Spontaneous recovery'],
 'Behavior strengthened by positive consequences and weakened by negative ones is the Law of Effect.');
TF('m4-oc','t','The Law of Effect states that behavior followed by a negative consequence is strengthened.', false,
 'False. Behavior followed by a negative consequence is weakened.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A coach gives a player a sticker every time she makes a good pass, and she makes more good passes. Which term fits?',
 ['Positive reinforcement','Negative reinforcement','Positive punishment','Primary reinforcer'],
 'Adding something to increase a behavior is positive reinforcement. A sticker is learned, not primary.');
Q('m4-oc','a','A driver speeds, and a loud warning alarm sounds in the car; afterward he speeds less often. The alarm is',
 ['positive punishment','negative punishment','negative reinforcement','positive reinforcement'],
 'Adding a stimulus (the alarm) to decrease a behavior is positive punishment.');
Q('m4-oc','c','How does shaping differ from reinforcing only the finished target behavior?',
 ['Shaping reinforces a series of closer approximations to the target','Shaping removes a pleasant stimulus to make a behavior happen less','Shaping pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus','Shaping relies on watching a model perform the target first'],
 'Shaping reinforces successive approximations, step by step.');
Q('m4-oc','c','Which is a primary reinforcer rather than a secondary one?',
 ['Water when thirsty','A paycheck','A gold star','Praise from a boss'],
 'Primary reinforcers need no learning. Money, stars, and praise work through learned associations.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A surgical resident practices suturing technique until her hand movements are precise. This skill belongs to the',
 ['psychomotor domain','cognitive domain','affective domain','semantic domain'],
 'Physical skills and fine motor movements are the psychomotor domain.');
Q('m4-obs','a','A patient knows the facts about smoking but still believes cigarettes help him relax and won\'t quit. Changing this belief is a goal in the',
 ['affective domain','cognitive domain','psychomotor domain','procedural domain'],
 'Beliefs, attitudes, and motivations are the affective domain, the hardest to change.');
Q('m4-forget','r','In normal aging, declarative memories',
 ['tend to decay','do not decay','become procedural','become implicit'],
 'Declarative memories tend to decay with age; procedural memory does not.');
TF('m4-forget','t','Procedural memory does not decay with normal aging.', true,
 'True. Declarative memories are the ones that tend to decay.');
Q('m4-forget','a','After a stroke reduces blood flow to part of her brain, a woman develops a lasting memory deficit. Which type of organic amnesia does this fit?',
 ['Disease/trauma','Retroactive','Proactive','Motivated'],
 'Strokes, decreased circulation, and Alzheimer\'s fall under the disease/trauma type.');
